in our series unifying america we are  recognizing efforts to right injustices  and bring communities together  pricey manhattan beach california is now  grappling with a very complicated racial  history  nearly 100 years ago a black family  owned a prosperous resort there  catering to people of color until the  city used a legal tactic to seize their  land and then just  dry them out as carter evan shows us a  plan is being unveiled today  that could return the land to the  family's descendants  manhattan beach is a favorite for  seeking a spot on the sand or a stroll  on the strand where homes can sell for  upwards of 20 million  but it looked a lot more like this in  1912 when willa and charles bruce bought  an ocean view lot  for around twelve hundred dollars they  were among the city's first  black landowners they had the bath house  here in the dining hall  dwayne shepard is the family historian  he says the l.a county lifeguard  headquarters now stands where willa  built  bruce's lodge it became a popular  destination for the black community when  segregation kept them off most beaches  so people weren't supposed to cross this  to go to the beach they had to walk a  half mile  in either direction before they could  get into the water barricades were built  tires were slashed and one real estate  agent even admitted he was trying to end  the quote  negro invasion there was a chapter of  the ku klux klan they started harassing  my family around  1920 they burned across they threw  burning mattresses under the porch of  one of the buildings  here were some black lives and they  didn't matter  a hundred years ago but i think they  matter now  the resort was right here it was right  here l.a county supervisor janice hahn  first learned what the bruce has endured  when the black lives matter movement  sparked conversations about  racism and income inequities i never  knew that we had a story right here in  manhattan beach  of an african-american family that  literally had their property stolen  from them city leaders ultimately used  eminent domain to shut down the bruce's  thriving business and make way  for a park it's a legal maneuver city  and county leaders now confirm  was racially motivated these people were  terrorized  and kicked out of a community where they  were trying to live peacefully  it really was an injustice to their  whole family generations  generations who surely would have been  millionaires  today if that beachfront property had  remained in the family  over the last century the city has grown  and prospered  but it ruled out reparations to the  family for lost income  that would be considered an illegal gift  of public funds  manhattan beach mayor suzanne hadley  says the council did  authorize spending 350 thousand dollars  on art to commemorate the family  and this week voted to acknowledge and  condemn what happened  but chose not to offer an official  apology it's awful it's wrong  we're not that community now i wouldn't  live here if we were a racist community  and my friends and neighbors would not  live here as well  last month a group calling itself  concerned citizens of manhattan beach  even took out an  ad in a local paper claiming some are  trying to create a racist  problem where there is none do you think  manhattan beach is a racist community  so we are less than one percent  african-american  that defines racism to me for some 20  years melissa clinton raised her kids in  manhattan beach  back in 2015 at two in the morning  someone threw a burning tire at the  family's front door  they escaped unhurt but the trauma  remains what it reminded me is that  things  haven't changed that much that terror is  still real  people who look like me are terrorized  we aren't entitled to  the comfort of security her neighbors  rallied around her so the family stayed  but clinton wonders what the city would  look like today  if bruce's beach had not been shut down  this community might be teeming with  black folks  if we had not destroyed that family it  changed the trajectory not only of their  lives  and their offsprings but of this  community to help usher in  change supervisor han teamed up with  state legislators to propose  giving the property back to the bruce's  descendants  the county would then lease the land  back from the bruces  creating income for the family i think  it's a moment  for manhattan beach i think it's a  moment for la county  that we could maybe be a model for the  rest of the country  do you ever think about what willa and  charles would have thought all this well  they would have loved it  i'm sure they're proud of us right now  for fighting to get that back  we're all proud of you after hearing  that story there was carter evans  reporting i mean  i love melissa where she said you know  burning tire was was thrown through the  home that doesn't actually say welcome  to the neighborhood  but the other neighbors did rally and  the fact that they want to rectify it  and  make it better and correct it says a lot  about that commitment everybody can  agree that the burning tire is a problem  but more people need to agree that the  historical  wealth gap is a problem and that goes  back to property property is wealth  and it cushions your life for  generations and this is an example of  that
 

